    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "36f70ba9_4fc9ae30",
        "filename": "bluetooth/socket/aidl/android/hardware/bluetooth/socket/RfcommCapabilities.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884477
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-24T17:54:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "@jaydenk from our discussion about the LE coc mtu configuration I remember that the maximum MTU needs to be provided by the offload stack to account for limitations in memory; that same limit will also apply to RFCOMM, and there is no reason why it should be different (give or take the RFC header bytes).",
      "parentUuid": "a25e1427_cd84c012",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dde33f2109c44206e2918231638781124aa520dd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "73c4aaf8_4d6fc4f9",
        "filename": "bluetooth/socket/aidl/android/hardware/bluetooth/socket/RfcommCapabilities.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1959861
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-25T05:18:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, I agree that it may be affected by the same memory constraints of the offload stack, but since the usage of LE CoC and RFCOMM are different, we may want to set them differently for each protocol. BTW, the `MaximumPacketSize` of the newly added Android API (BluetoothSocketSettings::setMaximumPacketSize) also can be used to differentiate between different protocols on BluetoothSocket. Then, do we have to move the field into the SocketCapabilities and set the valid range as RFCOMM\u0027s one because RFCOMM\u0027s range is narrower than L2CAP COC? I\u0027m curious, if a new protocol like ATT has quite smaller range like 500 bytes in offload stack do we have to reduce the common packet size as well?",
      "parentUuid": "36f70ba9_4fc9ae30",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dde33f2109c44206e2918231638781124aa520dd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ebb89ba4_5c13a622",
        "filename": "bluetooth/socket/aidl/android/hardware/bluetooth/socket/RfcommCapabilities.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884477
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-27T01:02:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Closing since this is not a strong requirement, but note that ATT is unlikely to use the same APIs, this is absolutely not a concern here.",
      "parentUuid": "73c4aaf8_4d6fc4f9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dde33f2109c44206e2918231638781124aa520dd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "04d6911a_866eec0b",
        "filename": "bluetooth/socket/aidl/android/hardware/bluetooth/socket/RfcommChannelInfo.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 66,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884477
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-22T22:10:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do you know how the maximum frame size is currently configured for RFCOMM ?\nIs there a relation to the MTU for the L2CAP channel or is it independent ?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 66,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dde33f2109c44206e2918231638781124aa520dd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8ead0491_f28e514a",
        "filename": "bluetooth/socket/aidl/android/hardware/bluetooth/socket/RfcommChannelInfo.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 66,
      "author": {
        "id": 1959861
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-22T22:44:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Currently, socket layer sets 990 bytes for RFCOMM max frame size.\n\n```\n#define BTA_JV_DEF_RFC_MTU (3 * 330)\n\n  if (RFCOMM_CreateConnectionWithSecurity(UUID_SERVCLASS_SERIAL_PORT, remote_scn, false, BTA_JV_DEF_RFC_MTU, peer_bd_addr, \u0026handle, bta_jv_port_mgmt_cl_cback, sec_mask)\n```\n\nThen, RFCOMM layer (port_api.cc) find minimum MTU between the input RFCOMM max frame size (3*330) and max L2CAP MTU (1691-1). The default socket RFCOMM max frame size is smaller so that 990 bytes has been used for RFCOMM socket for now.\n\nIn below code, `mtu` is 990 bytes socket layer configured. `rfcomm_mtu` is the calculated L2CAP MTU for RFCOMM based on hard-coded value.\n\n```\n  // Find MTU\n  // If the MTU is not specified (0), keep MTU decision until the PN frame has\n  // to be send at that time connection should be established and we will know\n  // for sure our prefered MTU\n  uint16_t rfcomm_mtu \u003d L2CAP_MTU_SIZE - RFCOMM_DATA_OVERHEAD;\n  if (mtu) {\n    p_port-\u003emtu \u003d (mtu \u003c rfcomm_mtu) ? mtu : rfcomm_mtu;\n  } else {\n    p_port-\u003emtu \u003d rfcomm_mtu;\n  }\n```\n\nSo, it sounds like RFCOMM max frame size will not larger than the calculated L2CAP MTU.",
      "parentUuid": "04d6911a_866eec0b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 66,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dde33f2109c44206e2918231638781124aa520dd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c9ff57dd_a0c4f26e",
        "filename": "bluetooth/socket/aidl/android/hardware/bluetooth/socket/RfcommChannelInfo.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 66,
      "author": {
        "id": 1959861
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-23T00:30:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In host stack implementation, there is relation to the hard-coded MTU of L2CAP as 1685 bytes (\u003d 1691 - 6). RFCOMM max frame size could be capped by the L2CAP MTU but socket layer already configured smaller 990 bytes so that 990 bytes has been used. Anyway, we need `maxFrameSize` field in RfcommChannelInfo.aidl",
      "parentUuid": "8ead0491_f28e514a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 66,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dde33f2109c44206e2918231638781124aa520dd",
